<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Hospitaller Caribbean colonies</h1><p class="st-meta">1651 CE – 1665 CE · capital Basseterre</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Caribbean islands owned by the Knights of Malta, a crusading order that briefly ran Saint Kitts, Saint Martin, Saint-Barthélemy and Saint Croix.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</a>, a Knight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-antilles">French Antilles</a>, persuaded the order to buy the French shares of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Saint Kitts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-martin-island">Saint Martin</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-barthelemy-swedish">Saint-Barthélemy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a> from the bankrupt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-des-iles-de-lamerique">Compagnie des Îles d'Amérique</a>. The purchase, approved by Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-paolo-lascaris">Jean-Paul Lascaris-Castellar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, was completed in 1651.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Poincy</a> governed the islands from his château near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basseterre">Basseterre</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Saint Kitts</a> as the order's lieutenant, while the French crown kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. It was the only overseas colony the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Hospitallers</a> ever held. The economy rested on tobacco and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, with enslaved Africans as labour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-barthelemy">Saint-Barthélemy's</a> settlement was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> in 1656. After Poincy's death in 1660, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-sales">Charles de Sales</a> succeeded him.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The venture never paid the distant order much. In 1665 the knights sold their rights to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a>, returning the islands to ordinary French colonial rule. The episode left little trace beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Poincy</a>'s buildings.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1651 CE – 1665 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.56%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1651 CE – 1665 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Basseterre</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy, Charles de Sales</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a>, Saint Barthélemy, Saint Martin, <a href="/countries/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Knights of Malta in the Caribbean, Hospitaller Saint Christophe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1658&amp;focus=hospitaller-caribbean-colonies" aria-label="Hospit